---
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import Slide from "../components/slide.astro";
import Figure from "../components/figure.astro";
import Lever from "../components/lever.astro";
---

<Slide prev="index" next="springtime">
    <!-- <Figure
        id="cameron"
        src="/images/www.cameronsworld.net.png"
        x="0.5"
        y="0.5"
        class="active"
        figure="5"
        width="50rem"
    >
        a `visage`[4] of the early web. This is a screenshot from `<a
            href="https://www.cameronsworld.net">cameronsworld.net</a
        >`, a postmodern <a href="aha">adventure</a> of the soul through gif art.[5]
    </Figure> -->
    <Figure
        id="wcaglette"
        src="/images/wcaglette.jpg"
        x="0.4"
        y="0.3"
        figure="12"
        width="30rem"
    >
        “You can’t meet WCAG without breaking a few eggs,” or so the saying
        goes.[72] Image courtesy of <a
            href="//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:Stripey_the_crab"
            target="_blank">Stripey the crab</a
        > (<a
            href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=12172841"
            target="_blank">link</a
        >).
    </Figure>
    <Figure id="zeno" src="/images/zeno.png" x="0.6" y="0.4" width="50em">
        <span
            style="width: 100%; display: inline-flex; justify-content: space-between;"
        >
            <span>
                <b>Figure 21:</b> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; Zeno! &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
                &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
                &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; Zeno!&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
                &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; Zeno! &nbsp; &nbsp;
                &nbsp; &nbsp; Zeno! &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Zen &nbsp; o &nbsp;en ! o
            </span>
            <span>
                (from <i
                    ><a
                        href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zeno%27s_paradoxes"
                        target="_blank">Wikipedia</a
                    ></i
                >)
            </span>
        </span>
    </Figure>
    <Figure
        id="dichotomy"
        src="/images/the-dichotomy.png"
        x="0.4"
        y="0.6"
        width="40em"
    >
        <span
            style="width: 100%; display: inline-flex; justify-content: space-between;"
        >
            <span>
                <b>Figure 22:</b> &nbsp; <i>The dichotomy</i>
            </span>
            <span>
                (from <i
                    ><a
                        href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=55976282"
                        target="_blank">Wikipedia</a
                    ></i
                >)
            </span>
        </span>
    </Figure>
    <Figure
        id="lerp-with-apples"
        src="/images/lerp-with-apples.jpg"
        x={0.6}
        y={0.5}
        width={"38em"}
        figure={23}
    >
        A nice picture explaining <i>Linear Interpolation</i>[4] that I found on
        Google Images. Created by Engineering Hack for youtube.com (<a
            href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QXpilXhDsNA"
            title="Youtube: Engineering Hack"
            target="_blank">link</a
        >).
    </Figure>
    <Figure id="fps-figure" x={0.618} y={0.618} class="act-i">
        <div id="fps-display" slot="figure">
            <figcaption>FPS:</figcaption>
            <output name="fps" data-live-display="FPS">00.0</output>
        </div>
    </Figure>
    <Figure id="w3c-raf" src="/images/w3c-raf.webp" x={0.5} y={0.5} figure={24}>
        From <i
            >Timing control for script-based animations (W3C Working Group Note
            22 September 2015)</i
        >. From <a
            href="https://www.w3.org/TR/animation-timing/"
            target="_blank">https://www.w3.org/TR/animation-timing/</a
        >.
    </Figure>
    <Lever
        class="act-i"
        startValue={0}
        name="fps-throttle"
        values={[Infinity, 34, 12, 6, 3, 1]}
    />

    <Figure
        id="half-life-larp"
        src="/images/half-life-larp.webp"
        x="0.4"
        y="0.6"
        width="40em"
        figure={25}
    >
        A <i>photo-montage</i> of fans of the video-game <i>Half-Life</i>™
        (1998). The game was ground-breaking for its time. “Environments are
        completely interactive,” gushed the <i>LA Times</i>. “Players may turn
        on the hand dryers.”[17]
    </Figure>

    <Figure
        id="spring-garlic"
        src="/images/spring-foreshadow-garlic.webp"
        class="act-i"
        x="0.4"
        y="0.35"
        width="35em"
        figure={48}
    >
        Wild garlic in the spring-time. From <i>National Trust Images</i> and Andrew
        Butler (<a
            href="https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/visit/countryside-woodland/places-to-forage-for-wild-garlic"
            target="_blank">link</a
        >).
    </Figure>
    <Figure
        id="spring-tigger"
        src="/images/spring-foreshadow-tigger.webp"
        class="act-i"
        x="0.5"
        y="0.5"
        width="25em"
        figure={49}
    >
        There’s something missing in my step...
    </Figure>
    <Figure
        id="spring-dog"
        src="/images/spring-foreshadow-dog.jpeg"
        class="act-i"
        x="0.7"
        y="0.3"
        width="48em"
        figure={50}
    >
        “Slinky’s laid-back outlook on life and flexible attitude make him one
        of the most sensible toys.” This is what the internet web-site <i
            ><a
                href="https://characters.disney.com/toy-story/slinky-dog"
                target="_blank">characters.disney.com</a
            ></i
        > says about <i>Slinky Dog</i>.
    </Figure>
    <Figure
        id="spring-slinky"
        src="/images/spring-foreshadow-slinky.webp"
        class="act-i"
        x="0.26"
        y="0.62"
        width="50em"
        figure={51}
    >
        “A spring, a spring, a marvelous thing! Everyone knows it’s Slinky!”[41]
        Heres a slinky story: a naval engineer named <a
            href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_T._James"
            target="_blank">Richard</a
        > invented it accidentally, in 1943, while trying to make something else.
        His wife <a
            href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Betty_James"
            target="_blank">Betty</a
        > came up with the name, and together they became slinky magnates.
        <br /><br />
        In 1960, Richard left Betty and their six kids to join an evangelical Bible
        translation company in Bolivia. Betty ran the slinky business for the next
        40 years, did a really good job, and was inducted into the <i
            >Toy Industry Hall of Fame</i
        > in 2001. Richard died in Bolivia in 1974. (Photo from <i
            ><a
                href="https://content.time.com/time/specials/packages/article/0,28804,2049243_2048651_2049036,00.html"
                target="_blank">Time Magazine</a
            ></i
        >.)
    </Figure>

